1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,601
The same Lego bricks can
build a car or a house.

2
00:00:03,601 --> 00:00:06,189
The same letters spell listen and silent.

3
00:00:06,189 --> 00:00:09,216
The same beads make two
very different necklaces.

4
00:00:09,616 --> 00:00:11,712
Same pieces, new build, new thing.

5
00:00:11,712 --> 00:00:13,040
Molecules do this too.

6
00:00:13,490 --> 00:00:15,163
Diya is revising at night.

7
00:00:15,163 --> 00:00:17,695
Her notes say ethanol is C two H six O.

8
00:00:17,695 --> 00:00:20,771
It is a liquid that boils
at seventy eight degrees.

9
00:00:21,121 --> 00:00:24,580
The next line says dimethyl
ether is also C two H six O.

10
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:25,799
But it is a gas!

11
00:00:25,799 --> 00:00:28,121
It boils at minus twenty four degrees.

12
00:00:28,121 --> 00:00:29,042
Same formula.

13
00:00:29,042 --> 00:00:30,903
How can they be so different?

14
00:00:31,403 --> 00:00:35,579
By the end, you will know the answer,
and every type of isomer in your

15
00:00:35,579 --> 00:00:36,479
syllabus.

16
00:00:36,911 --> 00:00:40,162
First, a molecular formula
only counts the atoms.

17
00:00:40,162 --> 00:00:44,562
Like a list of Lego bricks, it
does not show how they are joined.

18
00:00:44,912 --> 00:00:49,596
Compounds with the same molecular
formula but different properties are

19
00:00:49,596 --> 00:00:50,860
called isomers.

20
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:52,361
This is isomerism.

21
00:00:52,711 --> 00:00:54,334
There are two big families.

22
00:00:54,334 --> 00:00:57,972
In structural isomers, the atoms
are joined in a different order.

23
00:00:58,322 --> 00:01:01,516
In stereoisomers, the atoms are joined
in the same order, but they point

24
00:01:01,516 --> 00:01:03,033
in different directions in space.

25
00:01:03,383 --> 00:01:05,780
Structural isomers come in four types.

26
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:09,036
Chain, position, functional
group, and metamerism.

27
00:01:09,686 --> 00:01:13,317
Let's start on our molecule
bench with chain isomers.

28
00:01:13,317 --> 00:01:16,723
Take five carbon atoms
and twelve hydrogen atoms.

29
00:01:16,723 --> 00:01:18,105
C five H twelve.

30
00:01:18,455 --> 00:01:20,901
Join the five carbons
in one straight line.

31
00:01:20,901 --> 00:01:21,946
This is pentane.

32
00:01:21,946 --> 00:01:23,780
It boils at thirty six degrees.

33
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:25,080
Now watch.

34
00:01:24,994 --> 00:01:29,389
The carbon at the end hops off and
joins the second carbon as a branch.

35
00:01:29,789 --> 00:01:32,479
This is two methyl butane,
also called isopentane.

36
00:01:32,479 --> 00:01:35,461
The counter still reads five
carbons and twelve hydrogens.

37
00:01:35,861 --> 00:01:36,796
One more hop.

38
00:01:36,796 --> 00:01:39,688
Now the middle carbon holds
four carbons around it.

39
00:01:39,688 --> 00:01:42,485
This is two two dimethyl
propane, or neopentane.

40
00:01:42,885 --> 00:01:45,419
Same formula, three different skeletons.

41
00:01:45,419 --> 00:01:47,036
That is chain isomerism.

42
00:01:47,036 --> 00:01:50,020
The carbon chain changes,
straight or branched.

43
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:52,318
And the properties change too.

44
00:01:52,318 --> 00:01:56,790
More branches make a rounder molecule,
so the boiling point drops, to twenty

45
00:01:56,790 --> 00:01:58,883
eight and then about ten degrees.

46
00:01:59,533 --> 00:02:01,654
Now let's sort the other types.

47
00:02:01,654 --> 00:02:02,740
Step by step.

48
00:02:03,090 --> 00:02:03,990
Step one.

49
00:02:03,870 --> 00:02:07,382
Check that both compounds have
the same molecular formula.

50
00:02:07,382 --> 00:02:09,438
If not, they are not isomers.

51
00:02:10,188 --> 00:02:11,088
Step two.

52
00:02:10,921 --> 00:02:15,414
Same functional group, same chain, but
the group sits on a different carbon.

53
00:02:15,414 --> 00:02:17,620
Like moving a sticker along a ruler.

54
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:20,747
Propan one ol and propan
two ol are position isomers.

55
00:02:21,497 --> 00:02:22,397
Step three.

56
00:02:22,332 --> 00:02:24,464
The functional group itself changes.

57
00:02:24,464 --> 00:02:26,151
Ethanol has an OH group.

58
00:02:26,151 --> 00:02:28,810
Dimethyl ether has an
O between two carbons.

59
00:02:28,810 --> 00:02:30,896
These are functional group isomers.

60
00:02:30,896 --> 00:02:33,264
Propanal and propanone are another pair.

61
00:02:34,014 --> 00:02:34,914
Step four.

62
00:02:34,869 --> 00:02:39,307
Same functional group, but the carbon
groups on its two sides change.

63
00:02:39,307 --> 00:02:42,479
Methoxy propane and ethoxy
ethane are both ethers.

64
00:02:42,479 --> 00:02:44,236
This is called metamerism.

65
00:02:44,986 --> 00:02:47,218
Compare the last two side by side.

66
00:02:47,218 --> 00:02:49,054
In one, the group changes.

67
00:02:49,054 --> 00:02:51,474
In the other, only the sides change.

68
00:02:51,824 --> 00:02:52,930
Pause and think.

69
00:02:52,930 --> 00:02:54,953
Methoxy propane and ethoxy ethane.

70
00:02:54,953 --> 00:02:57,758
Are they functional group
isomers, or metamers?

71
00:03:00,958 --> 00:03:01,858
Metamers!

72
00:03:01,641 --> 00:03:02,748
Both are ethers.

73
00:03:02,748 --> 00:03:04,331
The O stays an ether O.

74
00:03:04,331 --> 00:03:07,155
Only the carbon groups
on its two sides change.

75
00:03:07,805 --> 00:03:09,735
Now the second family, stereoisomers.

76
00:03:09,735 --> 00:03:12,044
Here the atoms are
joined in the same order.

77
00:03:12,394 --> 00:03:13,938
Look at but two ene.

78
00:03:13,938 --> 00:03:18,132
Its double bond is stiff, like
two planks nailed together twice.

79
00:03:18,132 --> 00:03:19,358
It cannot twist.

80
00:03:19,758 --> 00:03:24,385
So the two methyl groups can sit on the
same side, called cis, or on opposite

81
00:03:24,385 --> 00:03:25,784
sides, called trans.

82
00:03:25,784 --> 00:03:27,603
These are geometrical isomers.

83
00:03:28,003 --> 00:03:30,800
Next, a carbon holding
four different groups.

84
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:35,106
Its mirror image looks the same, but
it will not fit on top, just like

85
00:03:35,106 --> 00:03:36,819
your left and right hands.

86
00:03:37,219 --> 00:03:39,068
These are optical isomers.

87
00:03:39,068 --> 00:03:42,735
You will study both types
in detail in later chapters.

88
00:03:43,385 --> 00:03:44,390
Remember this.

89
00:03:44,390 --> 00:03:46,257
Same atoms, different build.

90
00:03:46,257 --> 00:03:47,646
A different compound.

91
00:03:48,346 --> 00:03:50,036
Now, one worked example.

92
00:03:50,336 --> 00:03:52,975
Name the type of isomerism in each pair.

93
00:03:53,475 --> 00:03:55,572
Butane and two methyl propane.

94
00:03:55,572 --> 00:03:58,508
The chain changes from
straight to branched.

95
00:03:58,508 --> 00:03:59,603
Chain isomers.

96
00:04:00,353 --> 00:04:02,400
Butan one ol and butan two ol.

97
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:05,276
The OH moves from carbon
one to carbon two.

98
00:04:05,276 --> 00:04:06,427
Position isomers.

99
00:04:07,177 --> 00:04:09,105
Butan one ol and ethoxy ethane.

100
00:04:09,105 --> 00:04:11,390
Alcohol and ether, the group changes.

101
00:04:11,390 --> 00:04:12,890
Functional group isomers.

102
00:04:13,640 --> 00:04:15,594
Always check the formula first.

103
00:04:15,594 --> 00:04:19,010
The butanols and ethoxy
ethane are all C four H ten O.

104
00:04:19,010 --> 00:04:22,277
Butane and two methyl propane
are both C four H ten.

105
00:04:22,927 --> 00:04:24,389
Now, the exam trap.

106
00:04:24,739 --> 00:04:28,849
Some students draw butane with a bend
in the chain, and count it as a new

107
00:04:28,849 --> 00:04:29,749
isomer.

108
00:04:29,819 --> 00:04:30,719
Wrong.

109
00:04:30,401 --> 00:04:35,008
A bent drawing is the same molecule,
because single bonds can turn freely.

110
00:04:35,008 --> 00:04:36,620
Count the longest chain.

111
00:04:36,620 --> 00:04:41,304
If it is still four carbons in a row
with no branch, it is still butane.

112
00:04:41,954 --> 00:04:43,326
Where is this asked?

113
00:04:43,326 --> 00:04:47,385
Questions on spotting the type of
isomerism, and counting the structural

114
00:04:47,385 --> 00:04:48,803
isomers of a formula.

115
00:04:49,403 --> 00:04:50,662
Exam level now.

116
00:04:50,662 --> 00:04:54,899
How many structural isomers are
possible for C four H ten O?

117
00:04:54,899 --> 00:04:56,212
Pause and solve.

118
00:05:01,612 --> 00:05:03,308
The answer is C, seven.

119
00:05:03,808 --> 00:05:04,764
First the alcohols.

120
00:05:04,764 --> 00:05:08,227
Butan one ol, butan two ol, two methyl
propan one ol, and two methyl propan

121
00:05:08,227 --> 00:05:09,127
two ol.

122
00:05:08,725 --> 00:05:09,625
That is four.

123
00:05:09,973 --> 00:05:11,206
Then the ethers.

124
00:05:11,206 --> 00:05:15,205
One methoxy propane, two methoxy
propane, and ethoxy ethane.

125
00:05:15,205 --> 00:05:16,337
That is three.

126
00:05:16,837 --> 00:05:19,901
Four plus three gives
seven structural isomers.

127
00:05:20,501 --> 00:05:22,041
Here is your recap.

128
00:05:22,041 --> 00:05:23,624
Screenshot this card.

129
00:05:26,824 --> 00:05:29,010
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

130
00:05:29,410 --> 00:05:30,310
One.

131
00:05:29,847 --> 00:05:32,922
Why do ethanol and dimethyl
ether behave so differently?

132
00:05:33,722 --> 00:05:34,622
Two.

133
00:05:34,122 --> 00:05:37,130
What is the difference between
position isomers and metamers?

134
00:05:37,930 --> 00:05:38,830
Three.

135
00:05:38,517 --> 00:05:42,681
Why can but two ene have cis and
trans forms, but butane cannot?

136
00:05:43,581 --> 00:05:46,747
Next time: when a bond breaks,
where do its two electrons go?

137
00:05:46,747 --> 00:05:49,664
We find out in bond fission,
nucleophiles and electrophiles.
